//? Title: Volumetric segmentation from sparse and dense labels
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A volume needs a class at every voxel even when annotations cover only a few slices. This report checks3D U-Net and V-Net tensor derivatives and sparse/overlap objectives,then visibly fits complete compact models to noisy oblique ellipsoids and shows actual orthogonal held-out slices.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.7_segmentation/volumetric_segmentation.cpp`.

> The method

3D convolutional encoders learn volumetric features;learned up-convolutions and skip connections recover voxel resolution. Sparse weighted CE excludes unannotated voxels,while squared Dice trains dense foreground overlap. Explicit Adam and restored validation checkpoints keep the fitting and evaluation policies visible.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/volumetric_segmentation_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/volumetric_segmentation_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/volumetric_segmentation_concept.tikz > build/volumetric_segmentation_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/volumetric_segmentation_concept.svg build/volumetric_segmentation_concept.dvi > build/volumetric_segmentation_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
